Mathematical Background and Proofs: The AIADN Framework (v2)

## Autonomous Intelligence and Autonomous Distributed Network Orchestration for Martian Settlement

---

## Preamble

The **Autonomous Intelligence and Autonomous Distributed Network** (AIADN) is the cognitive orchestration layer of MSAT. It coordinates a heterogeneous fleet of autonomous agents — ASR excavation rovers, ARC structural fabricators, AWR welding units, and QSI-1 multi-purpose quadrupeds — across a geographically distributed, communication-degraded, resource-constrained Martian surface environment.

Version 1 of this framework established five core guarantees: greedy task allocation optimality, exponential belief consensus, Lyapunov-stable resource tracking, $k$-fault-tolerance, and bounded communication overhead. This version substantially extends the framework by addressing seven identified gaps in the prior treatment: the coupling between task allocation and physical deployment, the shift from classical to semantic communication, robust adaptive control under nonlinear and faulty dynamics, cascading failure risk in the task dependency graph, learning-based hybrid optimization, digital twin self-awareness, and formal verification of mission-critical safety properties. Each extension is developed with the same level of mathematical rigor as the original sections, and all nine sections together constitute a unified, internally consistent theoretical architecture.

---

## §A. Foundational Notation and Agent Model

**Definition A.1 (Agent Set).** Let $\mathcal{A} = \{a_1, \ldots, a_n\}$ be the finite set of $n$ autonomous agents. Each agent belongs to type class $\tau(a_i) \in \mathcal{T} = \{\text{ASR}, \text{ARC}, \text{AWR}, \text{QSI}\}$ with capability vector $\mathbf{c}_i \in \mathbb{R}^d_{\geq 0}$.

**Definition A.2 (Task Set).** Let $\mathcal{J} = \{j_1, \ldots, j_m\}$ be the set of open tasks, each characterized by requirement vector $\mathbf{r}_k \in \mathbb{R}^d_{\geq 0}$, duration $\Delta_k > 0$, priority weight $w_k > 0$, precedence set $\mathcal{P}_k \subseteq \mathcal{J}$, and location $\ell_k \in \mathbb{R}^2$.

**Definition A.3 (Global State).** The AIADN state at time $t$ is:

$$\mathbf{S}(t) = \bigl(\mathbf{x}(t),\ \mathbf{e}(t),\ \boldsymbol{\sigma}(t),\ \mathbf{q}(t)\bigr) \tag{A.1}$$

where $\mathbf{x}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{2n}$ is joint position, $\mathbf{e}(t) \in [0, E_{\max}]^n$ is energy, $\boldsymbol{\sigma}(t) \in \{0,1\}^n$ is health, and $\mathbf{q}(t) \in \{0,1\}^{n \times m}$ is the assignment matrix.

**Assumption A.1 (Bounded Heterogeneity).** $\|\mathbf{c}_i - \mathbf{c}_j\|_2 \leq \Delta_c$ for any two agents of the same type.

**Assumption A.2 (Intermittent Communication).** Link $(i,j)$ is active with probability $p_{ij} \geq p_{\min} > 0$, with maximum delay $\tau_{\max} < \infty$.

**Assumption A.3 (Bounded Independent Failure Rate).** Agent failures follow independent Poisson processes with rate $\mu_i \leq \mu_{\max}$. This assumption is relaxed to correlated cascading failures in §G.

**Assumption A.4 (Nonlinear Agent Dynamics).** Each agent $a_i$ has local dynamics:

$$\dot{x}_i = f_i(x_i, u_i) + \phi_i(x_i) + d_i(t) \tag{A.2}$$

where $f_i$ is the known nominal dynamics, $\phi_i$ is an unknown nonlinear perturbation satisfying $\|\phi_i(x)\| \leq \bar{\phi}_i \|x\|$ for known bound $\bar{\phi}_i$, and $d_i(t)$ is a bounded disturbance $\|d_i\| \leq \bar{d}$. This replaces the implicit linear dynamics assumption of v1.

