## §E. Semantic and Goal-Oriented Communication

### E.1 From Classical to Semantic Rate-Distortion

The v1 framework treated communication using classical Shannon rate-distortion theory, which treats all bits as equally valuable. In a heterogeneous multi-agent system, this is wasteful: an ASR excavation rover and an ARC fabricator have fundamentally different information needs. They do not need to reconstruct each other's raw sensor streams — they need to align on **task-relevant intent**: "Area A is clear," "Structural load at Node 7 is within tolerance," "I will reach Site B in 14 minutes." This distinction motivates a **semantic rate-distortion** framework.

**Definition E.1 (Semantic Encoder).** For each agent type $\tau \in \mathcal{T}$, define a **semantic encoder** $\phi_\tau : \mathcal{O}_\tau \to \mathcal{M}_\tau$, mapping raw observations $O_i \in \mathcal{O}_\tau$ to compressed semantic messages $M_i \in \mathcal{M}_\tau$, where $|\mathcal{M}_\tau| \ll |\mathcal{O}_\tau|$.

**Definition E.2 (Task-Relevant Distortion Metric).** For a receiver agent $a_j$ making decision $d_j(M_i)$ based on received message $M_i$, the **task-relevant distortion** is:

$$D_{\text{task}}(O_i, M_i) = \mathbb{E}\left[\Phi^*(O_i) - \Phi(Q | M_i)\right] \tag{E.1}$$

the expected loss in allocation quality from acting on the compressed message $M_i$ rather than the full observation $O_i$.

**Definition E.3 (Semantic Rate-Distortion Function).** The **semantic rate-distortion function** for agent $a_i$ of type $\tau$ is:

$$\mathcal{R}_{\text{sem}}(D) = \min_{\phi_\tau :\ D_{\text{task}}(\phi_\tau) \leq D} I(O_i; \phi_\tau(O_i)) \tag{E.2}$$

the minimum mutual information between raw observations and semantic messages needed to maintain task distortion below $D$.

**Theorem E.1 (Task-Oriented Semantic Rate-Distortion Bound).** *Let $\Omega_{ij} = I(O_i; A_j)$ be the mutual information between agent $i$'s observation and the optimal action of agent $j$. Then:*

$$\mathcal{R}_{\text{sem}}(D) \leq \Omega_{ij} - \psi_{\text{sem}}(D) \tag{E.3}$$

*where $\psi_{\text{sem}} : \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ is non-decreasing in $D$ with $\psi_{\text{sem}}(0) = 0$. Crucially, the semantic rate $\mathcal{R}_{\text{sem}}(D)$ is strictly less than the classical rate $\mathcal{R}_{\text{class}}(D) = I(O_i; \hat{O}_i)$ for any $D > 0$, since task-relevant compression discards all information irrelevant to the action space.*

**Proof.** By the data processing inequality, $I(O_i; A_j) \leq I(O_i; \hat{O}_i)$ for any reconstruction $\hat{O}_i$, since actions are a deterministic function of beliefs. The semantic encoder minimizes $I(O_i; M_i)$ subject to $D_{\text{task}} \leq D$. Since $D_{\text{task}}$ depends only on the task-relevant component of $O_i$, the optimal encoder can ignore all task-irrelevant variation in $O_i$, achieving rates strictly below the classical bound for $D > 0$. The bound (E.3) follows by computing the minimum achievable rate via the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker conditions of the constrained optimization (E.2), with $\psi_{\text{sem}}(D)$ as the Lagrange multiplier term. $\square$

**Theorem E.2 (Semantic Communication Rate Reduction).** *For AIADN with agent types $\mathcal{T}$, the semantic communication budget satisfies:*

$$\sum_{i=1}^n R_i^{\text{sem}} \leq \beta_{\text{sem}} \cdot \sum_{i=1}^n R_i^{\text{class}}, \quad \beta_{\text{sem}} = \frac{\sum_i \Omega_{ij}}{\sum_i H(O_i)} < 1 \tag{E.4}$$

*and therefore semantic communication achieves the same coordination distortion $D^*$ at strictly lower bandwidth than classical communication.*

**Proof.** From (E.3), $R_i^{\text{sem}} = \mathcal{R}_{\text{sem}}(D^*) \leq \Omega_{ij}$ for each agent. Classical rate satisfies $R_i^{\text{class}} \geq H(O_i) - \log|\mathcal{M}_\tau|$. Taking ratios and summing: $\beta_{\text{sem}} = \sum_i \Omega_{ij} / \sum_i H(O_i) < 1$, since $\Omega_{ij} = I(O_i; A_j) \leq H(A_j) \leq \log|\mathcal{J}| \ll H(O_i)$ for high-dimensional sensor observations. $\square$

**Corollary E.1 (AIADN Semantic Budget).** For $n = 50$ agents with $H(O_i) = 12$ bits and $\Omega_{ij} \approx 3.5$ bits (estimated from action space entropy $\log_2 |\mathcal{J}| = \log_2 30 \approx 4.9$ bits discounted by partial observability), the semantic communication rate is:

$$\sum_i R_i^{\text{sem}} \leq 50 \times 3.5 = 175 \text{ bits/round} \tag{E.5}$$

compared to the v1 classical budget of 325 bits/round — a **46% bandwidth reduction** at the same task distortion level. This is significant for the MSAT radio architecture, where spectrum is shared with telemetry, scientific instruments, and Earth uplink.

**Definition E.4 (Critical Semantic Rate Threshold).** There exists a minimum semantic rate $R^{\text{crit}}_{\text{sem}} > 0$ below which **intent alignment is structurally impossible**: agents cannot coordinate on any non-trivial task. From (E.3) with $D = D_{\max}$ (maximum tolerable distortion for safety):

$$R^{\text{crit}}_{\text{sem}} = \mathcal{R}_{\text{sem}}(D_{\max}) = \Omega_{ij} - \psi_{\text{sem}}(D_{\max}) \tag{E.6}$$

AIADN must maintain per-agent communication rate above $R^{\text{crit}}_{\text{sem}}$ as a hard constraint; failure to do so implies task coordination breakdown regardless of other system properties.

