## §F. Distributed Consensus and Belief Synchronization

**Definition F.1 (Belief Update Rule).** Agent $a_i$ updates belief $\mathbf{b}_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}^d$ as:

$$\mathbf{b}_i(t+1) = w_{ii}\mathbf{b}_i(t) + \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i(t)} w_{ij}(t) \cdot \mathbf{b}_j(t - \tau_{ij}(t)) + \mathbf{o}_i(t) \tag{F.1}$$

with doubly stochastic weights, bounded delay $\tau_{ij} \leq \tau_{\max}$, and local observation innovation $\mathbf{o}_i(t)$.

**Theorem F.1 (Exponential Belief Consensus).** Under Assumption A.2 and Lemma B.1:

$$\mathbb{E}\left[\max_{i,j}\|\mathbf{b}_i(t) - \mathbf{b}_j(t)\|_2\right] \leq C \cdot \rho^t, \quad \rho = 1 - p_{\min}\lambda_2(L^{\text{nom}}) \in (0,1) \tag{F.2}$$

**Proof.** Identical to v1 Theorem D.1; restated here for completeness. The disagreement operator norm is bounded by $\rho$ in expectation; iteration yields geometric convergence. $\square$

**Extension F.1 (Semantic Belief Compression).** Under the semantic encoder $\phi_\tau$ (Definition E.1), the belief update (F.1) is modified to operate on semantic messages $M_i = \phi_\tau(\mathbf{b}_i)$ rather than raw beliefs. The consensus dynamics then converge to a **semantic consensus** $\bar{M}$ satisfying $D_{\text{task}}(\bar{M}, \mathbf{b}^*) \leq D^*$, rather than exact belief consensus. Theorem F.1 continues to hold with $\|\mathbf{b}_i - \mathbf{b}_j\|$ replaced by $D_{\text{task}}(M_i, M_j)$, establishing **semantic consensus at the full convergence rate** of the original protocol.

