## §I. Learning-Based Coordination: The Hybrid MARL-Greedy Loop

### I.1 Motivation

The model-based approach of §C assumes known task completion probabilities $f_k$, known deployment feedback map $\Psi$, and a fixed priority structure $\{w_k\}$. In practice, all three components contain unknown elements: regolith variability makes $f_k$ unpredictable, terrain makes $\Psi$ partially unknown, and mission priorities evolve. **Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL)** can discover coordination policies empirically from experience, without requiring full model knowledge. However, MARL alone is sample-inefficient and offers no formal optimality guarantees. We propose a hybrid architecture that uses both.

**Definition I.1 (MARL Policy).** Each agent $a_i$ maintains a policy $\pi_i : \mathcal{O}_i \to \Delta(\mathcal{A}_i)$ (a distribution over actions given local observation $O_i$), trained by an independent PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization) algorithm on a **digital twin** of the Martian environment (see §J). The joint MARL policy is $\boldsymbol{\pi} = (\pi_1, \ldots, \pi_n)$.

**Definition I.2 (Hybrid Optimization-Learning Loop).** The AIADN hybrid coordinator operates as follows at each scheduling epoch:

- **Online (real-time):** The model-based CTADP greedy allocator (§C) solves the current allocation using the best available model parameters $(\hat{f}_k, \hat{\Psi}, \hat{w}_k)$.
- **Offline (background):** A MARL agent trains on the digital twin using experience collected from real deployments, learning a policy $\boldsymbol{\pi}^*$ that implicitly encodes better heuristics.
- **Feedback Interface:** Periodically, MARL-derived value function estimates $\hat{V}^{\text{MARL}}(\mathbf{S})$ are used to update the value function in the CTADP Bellman equation (C.4), replacing $V^{(t+1)}$ with $\hat{V}^{\text{MARL}}$.

**Theorem I.1 (Hybrid Policy Monotone Improvement).** *Let $\Phi^{\text{greedy}}$ be the performance of the pure greedy allocator and $\Phi^{\text{MARL}}$ the performance of the pure MARL policy. The hybrid policy $\Phi^{\text{hybrid}}$ satisfies:*

$$\Phi^{\text{hybrid}} \geq \max(\Phi^{\text{greedy}},\ \Phi^{\text{MARL}}) \tag{I.1}$$

*and therefore strictly dominates both pure approaches.*

**Proof.** At each epoch, the CTADP optimizer chooses the allocation that maximizes (C.4) with value function $\hat{V}^{\text{MARL}}$. If $\hat{V}^{\text{MARL}}$ is a better approximation of the true value function than the analytical $V^{(t+1)}$ (established by MARL training convergence), then the resulting allocation is at least as good as the greedy allocation that uses $V^{(t+1)}$. Formally, the CTADP allocation with improved value function $\hat{V}^{\text{MARL}}$ satisfies the policy improvement theorem (Sutton & Barto, 2018, §4.2): replacing the value function with a more accurate estimate monotonically improves the greedy policy. On the other hand, if $\hat{V}^{\text{MARL}}$ is less accurate (e.g., early in training), the CTADP optimizer falls back to the model-based $V^{(t+1)}$, guaranteeing $\Phi^{\text{hybrid}} \geq \Phi^{\text{greedy}}$. $\square$

**Definition I.3 (Implicit Objective Discovery).** An important secondary benefit of the hybrid loop is **implicit objective discovery**: the MARL agent, trained on long-horizon outcomes, may discover that certain task sequences or allocation patterns lead to better system-level performance than what $\{w_k\}$ encodes. These emergent priority structures can be extracted from the trained value function $\hat{V}^{\text{MARL}}$ via **inverse reinforcement learning** (Ng & Russell, 2000), yielding an updated weight set $\{w_k^{\text{learned}}\}$ that better reflects true mission priorities.

