## §J. The AIADN Digital Twin and Self-Awareness Architecture

**Definition J.1 (Digital Twin State).** The AIADN Digital Twin (AIADN-DT) is a real-time computational mirror of the physical fleet, maintaining a virtual state:

$$\hat{\mathbf{S}}(t) = \bigl(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(t),\ \hat{\mathbf{e}}(t),\ \hat{\boldsymbol{\sigma}}(t),\ \hat{\mathbf{q}}(t)\bigr) \tag{J.1}$$

updated at rate $f_{\text{DT}} \geq f_{\text{physical}}$ using sensor feeds, agent telemetry, and the dynamics model (A.2). The discrepancy $\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{DT}}(t) = \|\hat{\mathbf{S}}(t) - \mathbf{S}(t)\|$ is the **twin fidelity error**.

**Definition J.2 (Look-Ahead Verification).** Before executing an allocation $Q^{(t)}$ from the CTADP, AIADN-DT performs a **$H$-step lookahead simulation**: it applies $Q^{(t)}, Q^{(t+1)}, \ldots, Q^{(t+H)}$ (under the current model) to the virtual state $\hat{\mathbf{S}}(t)$ and checks whether the resulting trajectory satisfies:

- Safety constraints: $\hat{\mathbf{x}}$ remains in the safe operating region $\mathcal{X}_{\text{safe}}$.
- Energy feasibility: $\hat{e}_i(\tau) \geq E_{\min}$ for all $i, \tau \in [t, t+H]$.
- Task completion: critical tasks complete before their deadlines.

If any constraint is violated, the allocation is flagged and re-solved with an additional constraint encoding the detected violation.

**Theorem J.1 (Twin-Verified Allocation Safety).** *If the twin fidelity error satisfies $\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{DT}}(t) \leq \delta^*$ and the look-ahead simulation returns "safe" for a $H$-step horizon, then the physical system under allocation $Q^{(t)}$ remains safe for at least $H - T_{\text{drift}}$ steps, where:*

$$T_{\text{drift}} = \left\lfloor \frac{\delta^*}{\max_i \|\dot{\mathbf{S}}_i\|_{\max}} \right\rfloor \tag{J.2}$$

*is the time until the twin fidelity error grows to the safety margin width.*

**Proof.** Since $\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{DT}}(0) \leq \delta^*$ and the physical state evolves continuously at rate $\|\dot{\mathbf{S}}\|_{\max}$, the twin fidelity error satisfies $\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{DT}}(t) \leq \delta^* + t \cdot \|\dot{\mathbf{S}}\|_{\max}$ over any horizon (by Gronwall's inequality applied to the error dynamics). The lookahead simulation verifies safety with margin $\delta^*$; the physical trajectory remains within the safety region for all $t < T_{\text{drift}}$ steps before the fidelity error exceeds this margin. $\square$

**Definition J.3 (Anomaly Detection via Twin Divergence).** Define the **anomaly score** at time $t$ as:

$$\mathcal{A}(t) = \|\mathbf{S}(t) - \hat{\mathbf{S}}(t)\|_{\Sigma^{-1}} \tag{J.3}$$

the Mahalanobis distance between physical and twin states, weighted by the inverse covariance $\Sigma$ of normal operating variability. A cyber-physical attack or mechanical fault manifests as $\mathcal{A}(t) > \mathcal{A}_{\text{thresh}}$ — distinguishable from normal noise because attacks induce systematic, correlated deviations that are inconsistent with the twin's dynamics model. This provides **model-based intrusion detection** without requiring labeled attack data.

