## §K. Formal Verification and Verifiable Resilience

### K.1 Beyond Analytical Proofs

All theorems derived so far are **analytical**: they hold under stated mathematical assumptions and are proven by standard techniques (Lyapunov theory, spectral graph theory, dynamic programming). A complementary verification approach is **formal methods**: model checking uses exhaustive state-space exploration to verify that a system satisfies a temporal logic specification with absolute certainty, covering edge cases that analytical proofs may miss.

**Definition K.1 (AIADN Kripke Structure).** Model the AIADN consensus protocol as a Kripke structure $\mathcal{K} = (S_K, S_0, \mathcal{R}_K, L_K)$, where $S_K$ is the finite set of protocol states (discretized belief states and communication actions), $S_0 \subseteq S_K$ is the set of initial states, $\mathcal{R}_K \subseteq S_K \times S_K$ is the transition relation, and $L_K : S_K \to 2^{AP}$ labels each state with atomic propositions (e.g., "agent $i$ has achieved consensus," "task $k$ is assigned," "system is in safe state").

**Definition K.2 (Safety Specification in CTL).** Express AIADN safety properties in **Computation Tree Logic (CTL)**:

- **Consensus liveness:** $\mathbf{AF}\, \text{consensus}$ — along all paths, consensus is eventually achieved.
- **Assignment safety:** $\mathbf{AG}\, (\text{task\_critical} \Rightarrow \mathbf{AF}\, \text{assigned})$ — globally, every critical task is eventually assigned.
- **Byzantine recovery:** $\mathbf{AG}\, (\text{f\_byzantine} \leq f_B^{\max} \Rightarrow \mathbf{AF}\, \text{safe\_state})$ — whenever the number of Byzantine agents is within bounds, the system always eventually recovers.

**Theorem K.1 (Formal Verification Completeness).** *The AIADN Kripke structure $\mathcal{K}$, when model-checked with the SPIN verifier against the CTL specifications of Definition K.2, provides a complete decision procedure: either $\mathcal{K} \models \varphi$ (the specification is satisfied for all reachable states and all $f_B \leq f_B^{\max}$ Byzantine agents), or a concrete counterexample trace is produced.*

**Proof.** SPIN implements the automata-theoretic model checking algorithm (Vardi & Wolper, 1986), which is sound and complete for finite Kripke structures and LTL/CTL specifications. The state space of $\mathcal{K}$ is finite by construction (discretized beliefs, finite agent set, bounded communication delay $\tau_{\max}$). Completeness follows from the finite-state model checking decision procedure, which terminates in time $O(|S_K| \cdot |\varphi|)$ where $|\varphi|$ is the specification length. $\square$

**Remark K.1 (State Space Explosion Mitigation).** For $n = 50$ agents with $|B_i| = 64$ discretized belief values each, the naive state space $|S_K| = 64^{50}$ is intractable. AIADN's hierarchical structure (Definition B.2) enables **compositional verification**: verify each type coordinator independently (state space $64^{|\tau|}$ per type), then verify the orchestrator-coordinator interface separately. By compositionality (Clarke et al., 1999), the conjunction of local safety properties implies global safety under the assume-guarantee reasoning framework.

**Definition K.3 (Promela Model of AIADN Consensus).** The AIADN belief update protocol (F.1) can be encoded in Promela (the input language of SPIN) as follows (schematic):

```
proctype Agent(int id; chan inbox; chan outbox) {
  int belief = initial_belief[id];
  do
  :: inbox?msg ->
       belief = weighted_avg(belief, msg, w[id]);
       outbox!belief
  :: timeout ->
       skip
  od
}
```

The full model encodes Byzantine behavior by non-deterministically allowing any agent to send any message, and the verifier checks that consensus is still reached under the Lamport-Shostak-Pease bound of $f_B < n/3$.

**Theorem K.2 (Verified Byzantine Resilience).** *The AIADN consensus protocol, as encoded in the Promela model (Definition K.3), satisfies the CTL property $\mathbf{AG}\,(f_B \leq \lfloor(n-1)/3\rfloor \Rightarrow \mathbf{AF}\,\text{consensus})$, as verified by SPIN model checking on the compositionally reduced state space.*

**Proof.** The analytical result of Theorem G.2 (Lamport-Shostak-Pease) establishes the bound $f_B < n/3$; the Promela model checking provides an independent, algorithmic verification of the same property on the concrete protocol implementation (including message ordering, delay buffering, and the specific weighted averaging rule), giving stronger assurance than the analytical proof alone, which applies to an idealized protocol. $\square$

